@startuml claim-protocol-sequence
' Claim protocol — Sequence diagram (associated process)
' Source: next.md "Claims" section and kanban-check.md gate/claim rules
title Claim protocol — comment-ID arbitration
autonumber

participant "Session A\n(operator X)" as A
participant "Session B\n(operator X, another session)" as B
database "GitHub issue\ncomment stream" as GH

note over A, B
  GitHub has no atomic test-and-set.
  The comment stream is append-only,
  attributed, and totally ordered by
  comment ID, so it is the arbiter.
  Status and assignee are only the
  human-visible signal.
end note

A -> GH : read comments (check)
B -> GH : read comments (check)
GH -> A : unclaimed
GH -> B : unclaimed

A -> GH : Claiming: operator X, nonce A
B -> GH : Claiming: operator X, nonce B

A -> GH : re-fetch comments (verify)
B -> GH : re-fetch comments (verify)
GH -> A : both claims, with IDs
GH -> B : both claims, with IDs

A -> A : A has the lower comment ID — A wins
B -> GH : edit B marker to append (superseded)

A -> A : only now do the slow part
A -> GH : finish and post the result

note over A, GH
  A gate uses the same protocol with
  Processed: in progress — then a
  finalized Processed: acknowledgment.
  A finalized marker hides the gate.
  An in-progress claim older than 24h
  is abandoned and may be superseded.
end note
@enduml
